#slide[
  === Share mechanics, strike price, and the four other terms.

  - Each completed tranche earns options on 1% of Tesla's common stock outstanding as of January 19, 2018. Before the 2020 five-for-one and 2022 three-for-one splits, that 1% was 1,688,670 shares; fully vested, the Grant would be options on 20,264,042 pre-split~shares. #pause

  - The strike price was \$350.02, Tesla's closing price on January 19, 2018 --- \$23.33 adjusted for the two splits. #pause

  - The Grant also carried the *Clawback Provision*, the *Leadership Requirement*, the *M&A Adjustment* and the *Five-Year Hold Period*. Like the 2012 Grant, it expires after ten years, on January 20,~2028.
]

#slide[
  === The prior grants make "33 times larger" a real comparison.

  - Musk had two earlier Tesla plans, both equity linked. The 2009 Grant had a performance component; the 2012 Grant was entirely performance based. #pause

  - The 2009 Grant came in two parts, each offering options on 4% of Tesla's fully diluted shares at the grant date. The first vested on time: one quarter immediately, then one forty-eighth each month for three years, so long as Musk kept working at~Tesla. #pause

  - The second part was performance based, keyed to four Model S milestones --- engineering prototype, vehicle prototype, first production vehicle, and the ten-thousandth production vehicle --- to be met within four years or~forfeited. #pause

  - The 2012 Grant carried a grant date fair value of \$78 million. That figure is the denominator of the 33x comparison in the opening paragraph.
]

