// framing and the plaintiff's biography (drummer, Dawn of Correction, nine of 3.1 billion
// shares) are Mirvis slides 1-2 and his footnote 2. The January 2024 opinion carries no
// biography, no share count and no band; the bullet is background, not a finding.
#slide[
  === "Great cases like hard cases make bad law."

  - Holmes wrote the line in _Northern Securities Co. v. United States_, 193 U.S. 197 (1904), and it is the standard frame for this case: a derivative suit against Musk as controlling stockholder and against all the directors. #pause

  - The disposition matches the frame: a 200-page opinion of January 30, 2024, after a five-day trial, in which Chancellor Kathaleen St. J. McCormick ordered~rescission. #pause

  - The plaintiff himself: Tornetta was a drummer, once in the heavy metal band Dawn of Correction, and owned nine of Tesla's 3.1 billion shares in 2018 when he filed the suit.
]

// DOC-5, TM-05 sec. 3.1 [DECK-SOURCED, PARTIAL] and [KC-1], [KC-2]. Barzuza slide 14 states
// "nine months and ten meetings of the Tesla compensation committee" and "Musk achieved all
// targets" as facts; SOURCE 1 secs. 1.1, 1.5(a) and 1.8(iv) contradict both. Per the conflict
// rule the opinion controls, and this slide states its findings directly rather than
// correcting hers. Her maximum-value and grant-date-fair-value figures are accurate.
#slide[
  === What the record shows about the process and the milestones.

  #set text(19pt)

  - The process did run about nine months and touched ten meetings --- but those were *Board or Compensation Committee* meetings, not ten meetings of the compensation committee, and the count was the defendants' argument, not a finding in their favor. #pause

  - The court's answer to it: "The defendants' statistics, however, elide the lack of substantive work. Time spent only matters when well spent." Musk made last-minute changes before six of those ten meetings. #pause

  - On achievement, the opinion's affirmative finding is narrow: "Tesla hit the first three milestones, consistent with its projections, by September 30, 2020." The remedy then rests on the Grant sitting "unexercised and undisturbed." #pause

  - The economics are as advertised --- a \$55.8 billion maximum value and a \$2.6 billion grant date fair value --- but each tranche needs *two* milestones. A description keyed only to the \$50 billion increments leaves out half of every vesting~condition.
]

#slide[
  === Paying the CEO is the quintessential business determination.

  #quote(block: true)[A board of director's decision on how much to pay a company's chief executive officer is the quintessential business determination subject to great judicial deference. But Delaware law recognizes unique risks inherent in a corporation's transactions with its controlling stockholder. Given those risks, under Delaware law, the presumptive standard of review for conflicted-controller transactions is entire~fairness.] #pause

  - The plaintiff invoked that standard by arguing the plan was a conflicted-controller transaction.

  #callout["The plaintiff thus forces the question: Does Musk control~Tesla?"]
]
